{"type":"message","id":"22ed9ad4","parentId":"e8741669","timestamp":"2026-04-11T15:22:16.252Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Good, now I can see the skill-creator structure. Let me now plan the skill based on what I know:\n\n1. The WeChat data is in `F:\\Wechat shuju\\文案整理\\私聊\\TXT` with many files\n2. Format: `YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM:SS 'username' message`\n3. Each file is a separate chat conversation\n\nThe user wants to create a personal skill based on their WeChat chat records. This would be a skill that captures their personal style, preferences, language patterns, and knowledge from these conversations.\n\nLet me think about what this skill should do:\n- It should read WeChat chat records from the specified directory\n- It should analyze the user's personal style, language patterns, topics of interest\n- It could help generate responses in the user's style, or answer questions based on the chat history\n\n\n- The core purpose is to understand the user deeply enough to assist with writing tasks in their authentic voice\n\nI'll need to set up the skill structure properly: initialize it with the right naming, load the WeChat data as a reference file, and write the core SKILL.md to define how the skill analyzes and applies the user's personal style. The data format shows timestamped messages organized by conversation, so I should make sure the skill can parse and leverage that structure effectively.\n\nSince these are private conversations in Chinese, the skill should focus on understanding the user's language patterns, how they communicate, and what matters to them based on their actual chat history.\n\nThe main challenge is that the raw WeChat data needs processing before it can be useful. I should create a parsing script that extracts meaningful patterns and conversation insights from these files, which can then inform the skill's guidance. This way the skill doesn't just store the data—it actively helps generate content that matches how this particular user actually communicates.\n\nFor the structure, I'll keep it straightforward with the essential files: the skill definition, the parsed conversation data as a reference, and possibly some utility scripts for processing new chats. The key is making sure the SKILL.md is lean and practical, storing the actual conversation data separately so it can be updated as the user chats more.\n\nSince reading all the files takes time, I should ask the user what they're hoping to achieve with this skill—are they looking to create content that sounds like them, help with replying to messages, or something else? That'll shape how I structure both the parsing and the skill itself.","thinkingSignature":"6ba0c890c58ead5803b852fc6ec89f21f8bef516cf24589830a241d14c1fcbc7"},{"type":"text","text":"让我再多看几个文件了解完整的内容风格：\n"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_function_omda5dol8eiy_1","name":"exec","arguments":{"command":"powershell -Command \"Get-Content 'F:\\Wechat shuju\\文案整理\\私聊\\TXT\\私聊_11.txt' -Encoding Default\""}}],"api":"anthropic-messages","provider":"minimax-portal","model":"MiniMax-M2.7","usage":{"input":14418,"output":562,"cacheRead":32834,"cacheWrite":0,"totalTokens":47814,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1775920916009,"responseId":"06299a127cc185ed903513f5f8a9e1f5"}}
