{"type":"message","id":"523ce25e","parentId":"11db9e30","timestamp":"2026-04-11T15:25:15.075Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The filename `˽��_20�Ӵ�����.txt` is the garbled version of `私聊_20岁初中学历.txt`. The problem is the `read_text` is using UTF-8, but the actual content might not be valid UTF-8 because the file name itself contains characters that couldn't be decoded.\n\nWait, actually the issue is that the file content IS UTF-8, but the first line's username field contains a Chinese name that ends with a quote, which is confusing my regex. Let me look more carefully:\n\n```\n2023-12-20 21:09:43 '20岁初中学历'\n\n\n```\n\nThe username here is `20岁初中学历` (Chinese characters describing an age and education level). The message content follows on the next line, so the format is timestamp, then username in quotes, then the message on a separate line. But wait—the FAIL output shows the entire line being matched as the username, which means the closing quote isn't being found. Looking at the raw bytes, the `\\xe9\\x95\\xbf` sequence corresponds to the character `长`, so there's something about how the text is encoded that's causing the quote matching to fail.\n\nActually, I think I see the problem now. The regex `(.+?)` is non-greedy, so it should match the shortest content between quotes, but it's stopping at the first quote it encounters within the Chinese text itself—like the quote in `20岁初中学历'`. That's why the pattern isn't capturing the full username and message correctly.\n\n```\n\npython\npattern = r\"^(\\d{4}-\\d{2}-\\d{2} \\d{2}:\\d{2}:\\d{2}) '(.+?)' (.*)\"\n```\n\nThe issue is that non-greedy matching with `(.+?)` stops too early when there are embedded quotes in the content. I need to match everything between the first `'` after the timestamp and the last `'` before the message content starts, rather than stopping at the first quote character.\n\nLooking at the actual format, each message spans multiple lines—timestamp and username on one line, then the message content on subsequent lines until a blank line separates it from the next message. So the regex needs to capture the username between quotes on the first line and then gather all the content that follows until the blank line delimiter. I see the issue now - the closing quote after `学历` is being treated as the end of the username field because the pattern matches `.+?` followed by `'`. So `20岁初中学历'` gets captured as the username, leaving just the opening quote for the message part, which breaks the regex. I need a pattern that matches everything between the opening and closing quotes without treating embedded quotes as field terminators. The non-greedy `+?` is matching just the first `'` character after the timestamp instead of the full timestamp, which breaks the pattern entirely. Let me try a different approach to parse this correctly.","thinkingSignature":"9d9d00f931799e41a43b2e4e4a132ee3c77fb5971e69643a2e5ee880250ce194"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_function_zf4x3m9ky3q4_1","name":"write","arguments":{"content":"# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport re\n\ndata_dir = Path(r\"F:\\Wechat shuju\\文案整理\\私聊\\TXT\")\nf = list(data_dir.glob(\"*.txt\"))[1]\ncontent = f.read_text(encoding=\"utf-8\")\nprint(\"Filename:\", f.name)\nprint()\n\n# Try different patterns to understand the format\nlines = content.strip().split(\"\\n\")\nfor i, line in enumerate(lines[:15]):\n    print(f\"Line {i}: {repr(line)}\")\n","filePath":"C:\\Users\\zhang\\.openclaw\\skills\\wechat-personal\\scripts\\test_parse.py"}}],"api":"anthropic-messages","provider":"minimax-portal","model":"MiniMax-M2.7","usage":{"input":24347,"output":784,"cacheRead":32834,"cacheWrite":0,"totalTokens":57965,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1775921095302,"responseId":"06299ac58d70c5efc024dd7878c94ab2"}}
